---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { computeOverview } from '../lib/overview';
import { PROBLEM_STATS, OUTCOME_STATS } from '../lib/params';
import { sponsorTableData } from '../lib/overview-tables';
import { CARE_SCENARIOS, moneyRange } from '../lib/care';

const v = computeOverview('SCN-BASE', null);
const sponsor = sponsorTableData();
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL; // ends with '/'
---
<BaseLayout
  title="National Health Assurance: Story & System Dashboard"
  description="A guided, evidence-linked dashboard explaining the proposed U.S. National Health Assurance system, its twelve-year rollout, operating architecture, safeguards, and interactive cost and financing models."
>
  <main>
    <section class="card">
      <h2>The system today</h2>
      <p class="desc">American healthcare is not one system. It is four
        payment systems stapled together, with your coverage determined
        mostly by your job, your age, and your income. About 157 million
        people get insurance through an employer, who pays most of the
        premium out of what would otherwise be wages. Medicare covers ~67
        million people over 65 or disabled, funded by payroll taxes, general
        federal revenue, and enrollee premiums. Medicaid &amp;
        <abbr class="overview-acronym" title="Children's Health Insurance Program">CHIP</abbr>
        cover ~72
        million lower-income people, funded jointly by Washington and the
        states. Everyone also pays directly at the point of care:
        deductibles, copays, and uncovered bills totaled $506B in 2023. And
        ~26 million people have no coverage at all. Here is where the $4.87T
        came from and where it went. Hover any ribbon for the details.</p>
      <div class="flow-panel" style="max-width:560px;margin:14px auto 0">
        <div class="flow-title">Actual 2023 spending: $4.87T
          (<abbr class="overview-acronym" title="Centers for Medicare &amp; Medicaid Services">CMS</abbr>)</div>
        <div id="flow-today-solo"></div>
      </div>
      <details class="tableview"><summary>View the distribution as a table</summary>
        <div class="tbl-scroll">
          <table class="data" id="sponsor-table">
            <thead><tr>{sponsor.head.map((h) => <th>{h}</th>)}</tr></thead>
            <tbody>{sponsor.rows.map((r) => <tr>{r.map((c) => <td>{c}</td>)}</tr>)}</tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </details>
    </section>

    <section class="card">
      <h2>What's wrong, by the numbers</h2>
      <p class="desc">The problem is not that America spends heavily on health; every
        rich country does. It's that America pays far more and gets less
        coverage, less security, and far more overhead. Every figure below
        is sourced (citations in the
        <a href="https://github.com/mikehalvorson/US-National-Health-Assurance-System/tree/main/research"
           target="_blank" rel="noopener">research repository</a>).</p>
      <div class="tiles" id="problem-tiles">
        {PROBLEM_STATS.map((s) => (
          <div class="tile">
            <div class="value">{s.value}</div>
            <div class="label">{s.label}</div>
            <div class="range">{s.note}</div>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="card">
      <h2>The fixable part</h2>
      <p class="desc">Most of that dysfunction is structural rather than medical,
        which means it can be fixed by redesigning how money moves rather than by
        rationing care. Four levers do most of the work:</p>
      <ul class="lever-list">
        <li><b>Administrative consolidation.</b> One public payer and one
          claims rail replace ~900 insurers' duplicated overhead and the
          billing bureaucracy providers maintain to fight them. Recoverable
          savings estimates in the literature run $200 to $500B a year.</li>
        <li><b>Drug-price negotiation and public manufacturing.</b> The
          <abbr class="overview-acronym" title="Inflation Reduction Act">IRA</abbr>
          already proved government negotiation works
          (<abbr class="overview-acronym" title="Congressional Budget Office">CBO</abbr>-scored, 38–79%
          price cuts on the first ten drugs). Applied system-wide against
          prices 2.78× the international average, plus a public manufacturer
          for essential generics, modeled on Civica Rx's real $30-a-vial
          insulin. The model tests net price cuts of 25 to 55%.</li>
        <li><b>Hospital payment reform.</b> Global budgets replace per-claim
          billing, ending facility-fee markups, related-party extraction, and
          a slice of the $76–101B/yr in scored low-value care, while
          readiness funding protects ERs and rural service lines instead of
          letting them close.</li>
        <li><b>Front-door capacity.</b> A community diagnostic-treatment
          network and specialist e-consults absorb the routine care that
          currently lands in $2,453-average
          <abbr class="overview-acronym" title="Emergency Room">ER</abbr>
          visits and months-long
          specialist queues.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="note">The bet behind this design: these levers can pay for most of
        covering everyone with no bills. Whether that holds is
        what the model on this page computes; it is not assumed.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="card">
      <h2>The proposal: National Health Assurance</h2>
      <p class="desc">A single public system replacing employment-linked
        insurance entirely, the design this
        <a href="https://github.com/mikehalvorson/US-National-Health-Assurance-System"
           target="_blank" rel="noopener">dashboard</a>
        simulates:</p>
      <ul class="lever-list">
        <li><b>Automatic, continuous coverage</b> for every resident: no
          enrollment cliffs, no churn, no linkage to your job.</li>
        <li><b>$0 at the point of care</b> for covered medically necessary
          care; medical debt for covered care is prohibited (patient billing
          capped at 0.5% of encounters).</li>
        <li><b>National drug purchasing</b> through a real-time public
          pharmacy claims utility (replacing
          <abbr class="overview-acronym" title="Pharmacy Benefit Managers">PBMs</abbr>),
          plus a Public Medicines
          Corporation manufacturing essential generics.</li>
        <li><b>Hospitals on global budgets</b> under public-service charters,
          funded for readiness and essential service lines, not per-claim
          revenue.</li>
        <li><b>A four-type community diagnostic-treatment network</b>
          (the plan specifies at least 15,000 units, from pharmacy
          micro-clinics to rural enhanced and urban public-health units;
          the Physical Care chapter derives the need-based count) delivering
          same-day routine care.</li>
        <li><b>A structured specialist backplane:</b> complete workups and
          24-hour e-consults before in-person referrals, with urgency classes
          and protected urgent capacity.</li>
        <li><b>Expanded benefits phased in:</b> long-term care (home-first),
          behavioral health, dental, vision, hearing, and
          <abbr class="overview-acronym" title="Emergency Medical Services">EMS</abbr>.</li>
        <li><b>A 12-year gated rollout (2027–2038)</b> with go/no-go
          phase gates, transition protections for patients, providers, and
          displaced workers, and independent oversight bodies.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="card overview-map-card">
      <span class="overview-kicker">The whole design in one view</span>
      <h2>The plan is one operating system, not a collection of separate programs</h2>
      <p class="desc">A universal public promise sits above a local care network.
        National payment, data, medicine, and workforce systems make that promise
        operable; measurable quality and independent governance keep every layer
        accountable. Select a chapter to inspect the detailed model.</p>

      <div class="overview-system-map"
        aria-label="Layered map of the National Health Assurance operating system">
        <div class="overview-promise-band">
          <span>Public promise</span>
          <strong>Automatic coverage · medically necessary care without a bill ·
            portable rights and appeals</strong>
          <a href={base + 'legislation'}>Enabling law</a>
        </div>
        <div class="overview-map-connector" aria-hidden="true">
          <i></i><span>turn the promise into care</span><i></i>
        </div>
        <div class="overview-care-band">
          <article>
            <span>Front door</span>
            <h3>Community diagnostic-treatment units</h3>
            <p>Same-day routine care, testing, prevention, navigation, and
              escalation close to where people live.</p>
            <a href={base + 'units'}>Physical Care</a>
          </article>
          <article>
            <span>Clinical backplane</span>
            <h3>Specialists and hospitals</h3>
            <p>Complete workups, e-consults, urgency routing, protected capacity,
              and hospital global budgets.</p>
            <a href={base + 'health'}>Healthcare</a>
          </article>
          <article>
            <span>Continuity</span>
            <h3>Home, community, and expanded benefits</h3>
            <p>Home-first long-term care plus behavioral, dental, vision, hearing,
              emergency, and public-health services.</p>
            <a href={base + 'rollout'}>Rollout</a>
          </article>
        </div>
        <div class="overview-map-connector" aria-hidden="true">
          <i></i><span>shared national backbone</span><i></i>
        </div>
        <div class="overview-backbone-band">
          <article>
            <b>Payment &amp; financing</b>
            <p>One public claims rail, global budgets, existing public spending,
              employer contributions, and progressive revenue.</p>
            <a href={base + 'tax'}>Financing</a>
          </article>
          <article>
            <b>Data &amp; cyber</b>
            <p>Portable records, authoritative nodes, common interfaces, audit
              trails, segmentation, and tested downtime paths.</p>
            <a href={base + 'data'}>Data</a>
          </article>
          <article>
            <b>Medicines &amp; supply</b>
            <p>National purchasing, a public pharmacy utility, transparent
              formularies, public production, and shortage response.</p>
            <a href={base + 'medications'}>Medication strategy</a>
          </article>
          <article>
            <b>Workforce &amp; education</b>
            <p>Role-region staffing plans, training expansion, rural service,
              travel teams, and a protected transition for displaced workers.</p>
            <a href={base + 'workforce'}>Workforce</a>
          </article>
        </div>
        <div class="overview-assurance-band">
          <span>Independent assurance across every layer</span>
          <div>
            <a href={base + 'quality'}>430 controlled parameters</a>
            <a href={base + 'gov'}>Distributed governance</a>
            <a href={base + 'hardening'}>Executive hardening</a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="card">
      <span class="overview-kicker">What changes for a patient</span>
      <h2>One person, one continuous care pathway</h2>
      <p class="desc">The system moves expertise and information toward the
        patient first. It moves the patient only when hands-on specialty or
        hospital care is necessary.</p>
      <div class="overview-care-path" aria-label="Patient care pathway">
        <article>
          <span>1</span>
          <h3>Covered automatically</h3>
          <p>Coverage follows residency, not a job, age band, or annual plan choice.</p>
        </article>
        <i aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</i>
        <article>
          <span>2</span>
          <h3>Enter locally</h3>
          <p>A nearby unit examines, tests, treats, and assembles a complete record.</p>
        </article>
        <i aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</i>
        <article>
          <span>3</span>
          <h3>Route by need</h3>
          <p>An e-consult can advise the local team or move the case into a protected queue.</p>
        </article>
        <i aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</i>
        <article>
          <span>4</span>
          <h3>Treat at the right level</h3>
          <p>Specialists, hospitals, and regional services take cases that require them.</p>
        </article>
        <i aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</i>
        <article>
          <span>5</span>
          <h3>Close the loop</h3>
          <p>Results, medications, follow-up, home support, and appeals remain visible.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
      <div class="overview-inline-links">
        <a href={base + 'health'}>See benefits and cost</a>
        <a href={base + 'units'}>See the local network</a>
        <a href={base + 'data'}>See how information follows the patient</a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="card">
      <span class="overview-kicker">How the money changes route</span>
      <h2>Replace fragmented collection and billing with public financing and simple payment</h2>
      <p class="desc">The proposal does not make healthcare free to produce.
        It replaces premiums, deductibles, point-of-care bills, and payer-specific
        administration with a publicly financed system that pays providers
        through a common claims rail and hospital global budgets.</p>
      <div class="overview-money-shift" aria-label="High-level money-flow change">
        <div class="overview-money-world today">
          <span>Today</span>
          <strong>Households + employers + governments</strong>
          <i aria-hidden="true">&darr;</i>
          <p>Premiums · taxes · deductibles · copays · uncovered bills</p>
          <i aria-hidden="true">&darr;</i>
          <b>Many insurers, networks, contracts, and billing rules</b>
        </div>
        <div class="overview-money-pivot" aria-hidden="true">
          <span>consolidate</span>
          <b>&rarr;</b>
        </div>
        <div class="overview-money-world proposal">
          <span>National Health Assurance</span>
          <strong>Existing public funds + employer contribution + progressive revenue</strong>
          <i aria-hidden="true">&darr;</i>
          <p>National health trust and public payment operations</p>
          <i aria-hidden="true">&darr;</i>
          <b>Claims payments · hospital budgets · unit funding · benefits</b>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="overview-inline-links">
        <a href={base + 'health'}>Test total system cost</a>
        <a href={base + 'tax'}>Test who pays and who saves</a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="card">
      <span class="overview-kicker">Implementation, not a switch-flip</span>
      <h2>Twelve years of staged authority, infrastructure, coverage, and benefits</h2>
      <p class="desc">Dates are anchors, not automatic deadlines. Eight independent
        readiness gates can hold, resize, or roll back a wave when claims,
        capacity, workforce, fiscal, artificial-intelligence, cyber, legitimacy,
        or continuity evidence is inadequate.</p>
      <div class="overview-rollout-arc" aria-label="Twelve-year high-level rollout">
        <article>
          <span>Years 1–2 · P0–P1</span>
          <strong>Found</strong>
          <p>Pass the statute; stand up accountable bodies, rights, identity,
            registries, cyber baselines, and transition protection.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span>Years 3–4 · P2–P3</span>
          <strong>Operate first rails</strong>
          <p>Launch pharmacy purchasing and production, then begin the first
            public coverage wave and claims operation.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span>Years 5–7 · P4–P5</span>
          <strong>Pilot and scale delivery</strong>
          <p>Test hospital budgets and all four unit types; expand workforce,
            specialist routing, and the care network.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span>Years 8–9 · P6</span>
          <strong>Make coverage the default</strong>
          <p>Scale public coverage, payment, rights, regional operations, and
            major cost-sharing elimination nationally.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span>Years 10–12 · P7–P8</span>
          <strong>Expand and certify</strong>
          <p>Add long-term care and other benefits only after capacity gates,
            then test the mature system against every performance floor.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
      <div class="overview-gate-line">
        <strong>Eight stop-the-clock gates</strong>
        <span>claims</span><span>unit capacity</span><span>workforce</span>
        <span>fiscal</span><span><abbr class="overview-acronym" title="Artificial Intelligence">AI</abbr> safety</span><span>records &amp; cyber</span>
        <span>legitimacy</span><span>continuity</span>
      </div>
      <div class="overview-inline-links">
        <a href={base + 'rollout'}>Open the complete phased rollout</a>
        <a href={base + 'quality'}>Inspect phase targets</a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="card" id="hero-card">
      <h2>The system-level price tag</h2>
      <p class="desc">This model's own projection, computed bottom-up from
        sourced parameters: what the fully-mature system would cost at today's
        (2024) scale, directly comparable with the ~$5.3T the U.S. actually
        spends now. The savings levers (drug negotiation, administrative
        consolidation, payment reform) largely pay for the benefit expansions
        (universal coverage, $0 care, long-term care, dental/vision/hearing,
        behavioral health).</p>
      <div class="hero-pair-label">If the mature system existed today, at 2024 scale</div>
      <div class="hero">
        <div class="hero-block">
          <div class="label">NHA, fully mature (median)</div>
          <div class="hero-num" id="hero-value">{v.heroValue}</div>
          <div class="range" id="hero-range">{v.heroRange}</div>
        </div>
        <div class="hero-block">
          <div class="label">What the U.S. actually spends, 2024</div>
          <div class="hero-num" style="color:var(--text-secondary)">$5.3T/yr</div>
          <div class="range">CMS preliminary</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="hero-pair-label">In 2041, at 2041's size (still measured in 2024 dollars)</div>
      <div class="hero">
        <div class="hero-block">
          <div class="label">NHA at steady state (median)</div>
          <div class="hero-num" id="hero-2041-nha">{v.nha2041}</div>
          <div class="range" id="hero-2041-range">{v.hero2041Range}</div>
        </div>
        <div class="hero-block">
          <div class="label">Status quo, same year</div>
          <div class="hero-num" style="color:var(--text-secondary)" id="hero-2041-base">{v.base2041}</div>
          <div class="range">CMS's own trajectory, no reform; the country is bigger and older either way</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="takeaway" id="family-burden-note">{v.familyNote}</div>
      <div class="tiles" id="tiles">
        {v.tiles.map((it) => (
          <div class="tile">
            <div class="label">{it.label}</div>
            <div class="value">{it.value}</div>
            <div class="range">{it.range}</div>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
      <p class="note"><b>Reading the pairs:</b> each row compares like with
        like. The first row asks what the mature system would cost if it
        existed today; the second asks what the annual bill looks like in
        2041, when the country is bigger and older under either system.
        Never compare across rows: the jump from ~$5.3T to ~$9T happens with
        or without reform, and it is more people, older people, and more
        care per person, not inflation. Every dollar on this page is in 2024
        purchasing power.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="card">
      <h2>Assumptions</h2>
      <p class="desc">Pick a stress scenario from the 19-scenario
        catalog, or adjust individual assumptions. Badges show evidence quality:
        <span class="conf high">high</span> = official statistic or scored estimate,
        <span class="conf medium">medium</span> = reputable study or survey,
        <span class="conf low">low</span> = analyst assumption with no direct source.</p>
      <div class="controls" id="controls"></div>
      <p class="scenario-desc" id="scenario-desc"></p>
      <div class="controls-foot">
        <button class="btn" id="reset-btn">Reset assumptions</button>
        <span class="runs-note" id="runs-note"></span>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="card">
      <h2>Annual total system cost, 2027–2042</h2>
      <p class="desc">The status-quo path follows CMS's own spending trajectory.
        The NHA path phases in over the Phase 0–8 roadmap
        (coverage waves from Year 4, national default coverage Year 8, expanded
        long-term-care/behavioral/dental benefits Years 10–12), including all
        transition costs. The band is the 10th–90th percentile across Monte
        Carlo draws of every uncertain parameter.</p>
      <div id="path-chart"></div>
      <p class="note" id="growth-decomp"></p>
      <details class="tableview"><summary>View as table</summary>
        <div class="tbl-scroll"><table class="data" id="path-table"></table></div>
      </details>
    </section>

    <section class="card">
      <h2>How the money re-routes</h2>
      <p class="desc">The most misunderstood part of a public
        system: society's total spending barely changes; <em>the routes
        change</em>. Compare the flow you saw at the top of the page with the
        same flow under NHA. Premiums and point-of-care bills disappear; the
        same dollars reach the same doctors and hospitals through public
        financing instead. Hover any ribbon for what it contains.</p>
      <div class="flow-grid">
        <div class="flow-panel">
          <div class="flow-title">Today: actual 2023 spending ($4.87T, CMS)</div>
          <div id="flow-today"></div>
        </div>
        <div class="flow-panel">
          <div class="flow-title" id="flow-nha-title">Under NHA: mature system at 2024 scale</div>
          <div id="flow-nha"></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="takeaway" id="flow-takeaway"></div>
    </section>

    <section class="card">
      <h2>Who pays</h2>
      <p class="desc">Mature-year public cost funded by redirecting what
        government already spends, a state maintenance-of-effort contribution,
        an employer contribution replacing today's premiums, and the remainder,
        which requires new revenue. The comparison shows how far the
        extreme-wealth tax package goes toward that gap.</p>
      <div id="financing-chart"></div>
      <p class="note" id="financing-note"></p>
      <details class="tableview"><summary>View as table</summary>
        <div class="tbl-scroll"><table class="data" id="financing-table"></table></div>
      </details>
    </section>

    <section class="card">
      <h2>Why the total lands where it does</h2>
      <p class="desc">The bridge from the status-quo baseline at maturity (2041)
        to the NHA total, using central-estimate parameters. Each saving
        mechanism appears exactly once: the model computes both worlds directly
        and derives these differences, so nothing can be double-counted.</p>
      <div id="bridge-chart"></div>
      <details class="tableview"><summary>View as table</summary>
        <div class="tbl-scroll"><table class="data" id="bridge-table"></table></div>
      </details>
    </section>

    <section class="card benchmark-section">
      <h2>Does the model pass an outside reality check?</h2>
      <p class="desc">Internal tests can show that the arithmetic works. They
        cannot show whether the result is plausible. These checks compare the
        model with observed U.S. spending and independent estimates of
        single-payer systems. Similar scale supports plausibility; it does not
        prove that any one outcome will occur.</p>

      <div class="benchmark-purpose-grid">
        <article>
          <span>Check 1</span>
          <strong>The nation&apos;s total healthcare bill</strong>
          <p>What would households, employers, states, and the federal
            government spend in total? This tests the overall size of the
            model.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span>Check 2</span>
          <strong>The shift onto the federal budget</strong>
          <p>How much additional spending would appear on the federal ledger?
            This tests the scale of the financing transfer.</p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <div class="benchmark-distinction">
        <strong>Keep the two checks separate.</strong>
        Added federal spending is not the same as added national healthcare
        spending. Much of the federal increase replaces premiums,
        point-of-care bills, employer spending, and state spending that already
        exist elsewhere in the system.
      </div>

      <article class="benchmark-panel">
        <div class="benchmark-panel-head">
          <span>Check 1</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Is the total system price tag in a credible range?</h3>
            <p>The dashboard&apos;s mature system at today&apos;s population and
              economy is compared with what the United States actually spent
              in 2024. Both figures include all payers.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="benchmark-key" aria-label="Chart key">
          <span><i class="benchmark-key-model"></i>Dashboard model range and median</span>
          <span><i class="benchmark-key-outside"></i>Observed or outside estimate</span>
        </div>
        <div id="benchmark-nhe"></div>
        <p class="benchmark-readout">
          <strong>Readout:</strong>
          <span id="benchmark-nhe-result"></span>
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="benchmark-panel">
        <div class="benchmark-panel-head">
          <span>Check 2</span>
          <div>
            <h3>How does the federal financing shift compare?</h3>
            <p>The dashboard&apos;s added federal spending is compared with CBO,
              Urban Institute, and Mercatus estimates. This is a budget
              comparison, not a measure of new cost to society or the exact
              amount of new tax revenue required. The sources use different
              years, benefit designs, and transition speeds, so this check is
              intentionally approximate.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="benchmark-figure-grid">
          <article>
            <span>Dashboard model</span>
            <strong id="benchmark-fed-model">...</strong>
            <small id="benchmark-fed-model-range"></small>
            <p>Mature system, 2041-scale economy, real 2024 dollars</p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <span>CBO <i class="conf high">high</i></span>
            <strong>$1.5T–$3.0T/yr</strong>
            <small>Federal subsidy increase</small>
            <p>Illustrative single-payer options in 2030, study dollars</p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <span>Urban Institute / Mercatus <i class="conf medium">medium</i></span>
            <strong>$3.2T–$3.4T/yr</strong>
            <small>Annualized federal increase</small>
            <p>First-decade estimates, each study&apos;s own dollars</p>
          </article>
        </div>
        <p class="benchmark-readout">
